0,-4.768370445162873e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.4379872977733612,-0.04321833699941635,-5.125986263010418e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,641,374,436,346,642,21495,400,25411,54876,9210,400,33,1706,3,323,9210,400,4897,32,3,6629,220,18,20,12348,13,3555,374,279,6629,315,9210,400,34,6352,3,30,508,6405,921,474,297,13842,62944,26,1159,17047,26,1379,7,16,17,20,1215,1638,2752,2333,258,28357,7,15,13,23,1106,7633,86154,1188,15,11,15,701,16,1106,16119,20912,37,3,44970,15,11,15,701,3741,4080,24,15,1106,12670,362,284,5419,7,16,21,15,701,425,284,5419,7,16,16,15,701,356,284,5419,7,19,15,701,422,284,5419,7,16,15,317,7633,4346,313,35,313,34,313,32,313,33,313,34,317,1502,20912,32,73315,32,49540,1215,2383,20912,33,73315,33,22037,1215,2383,20912,34,73315,34,42125,1215,2383,20912,35,73315,35,42125,317,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn isosceles triangle $ABC$, angle $BAC$ and angle $BCA$ measure 35 degrees. What is the measure of angle $CDA$? [asy]\nimport olympiad; import geometry; size(125); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));\ndraw(circle((0,0),1));\ndot(\"$F$\",(0,0),dir(-90));\npair A = dir(160), B = dir(110), C = dir(40), D = dir(10);\ndraw(A--D--C--A--B--C);\nlabel(\"$A$\",A,W); label(\"$B$\",B,N); label(\"$C$\",C,E); label(\"$D$\",D,E);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"127b1ffef7a3bdec41382bbd4863df29f19b9f3d483fb27276d96a063b34fcc0"},"seconds":21.883268356323242,"seed":264411249216003208,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we first need to identify and understand the given geometric configuration and the relationships among the angles in the isosceles triangle \\(ABC\\) and the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ADBC\\).\n\n1. **Identify angles in triangle \\(ABC\\):**\n   - Given that \\(\\angle BAC = 35^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle BCA = 35^\\circ\\), and knowing that the sum of the angles in a triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\), we can find \\(\\angle ABC\\).\n   - \\[\n   \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC - \\angle BCA = 180^\\circ - 35^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 110^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n2. **Understand the cyclic nature and angle properties:**\n   - The points \\(A, B, C, D\\) lie on the circumference of a circle, making \\(ADBC\\) a cyclic quadrilateral.\n   - In a cyclic quadrilateral, the opposite angles sum up to \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, \\(\\angle ABC + \\angle ADC = 180^\\circ\\).\n\n3. **Calculate \\(\\angle ADC\\):**\n   - Since \\(\\angle ABC = 110^\\circ\\), we can find \\(\\angle ADC\\) as follows:\n   - \\[\n   \\angle ADC = 180^\\circ - \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - 110^\\circ = 70^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\(CDA\\) is \\(\\boxed{70}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{70}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
